+++
title: Saqqara
description: Burial ground in Giza Governorate, Egypt
hook: Saqqara holds the oldest complete stone building complex known in
  history, the Pyramid of Djoser. Another sixteen Egyptian kings built
  pyramids here, and the place went on taking non-royal burials and cult
  ceremonies for more than 3,000 years, well into Ptolemaic and Roman times.
standfirst: An Egyptian village some 30 km (19 mi) south of modern-day Cairo,
  and the necropolis of the ancient capital Memphis. Across around 7 by 1.5 km
  lie royal pyramids, mastaba tombs and the private monuments high officials
  added through the entire Pharaonic period, in use well into Ptolemaic and
  Roman times.
key-facts:
  - name: What it is
    value: A necropolis
    from: "type: Necropolis"
  - name: In use
    value: From the Early Dynastic Period to the Middle Ages
    from: "periods: Early Dynastic Period to Middle Ages"
  - name: Where
    value: Giza Governorate, Egypt, in Lower Egypt
    from: "location: Giza Governorate, Egypt region: Lower Egypt"
  - name: How far from Cairo
    value: Some 30 km (19 mi) south of modern-day Cairo
    from: Located some 30 km (19 mi) south of modern-day [[ Cairo ]], Saqqara
      covers an area of around 7 by 1.5 km (4.3 by 0.9 mi).
  - name: How large
    value: Around 7 by 1.5 km (4.3 by 0.9 mi)
    from: Saqqara covers an area of around 7 by 1.5 km (4.3 by 0.9 mi).
  - name: World Heritage listing
    value: Inscribed in 1979 (3rd Session); the listed area is 16,203.36 ha
      (62.5615 sq mi)
    from: "inscription: 1979 (3rd Session) area: 16,203.36 ha (62.5615 sq mi)"
pull-quotes:
  - text: The finding of this mask could be called a sensation. Very few masks of
      precious metal have been preserved to the present day, because the tombs
      of most Ancient Egyptian dignitaries were looted in ancient times.
    attribution: Ramadan Badry Hussein, of the University of Tübingen
    section: 2010s
  - text: Most of the mummies we discovered last season were very modest, they
      were only subjected to basic embalming treatments, wrapped in bandages
      and placed directly in pits dug in the sand
    attribution: Kamil Kuraszkiewicz
    section: 2010s
  - text: This discovery is very important because it proves that Saqqara was the
      main burial of the 26th Dynasty
    attribution: Zahi Hawass, an Egyptologist and Egypt's former Minister of State
      for Antiquities Affairs
    section: 2020s
timeline:
  - when: 1842 and 1843
    what: Karl Richard Lepsius, on a Prussian expedition, studied, mapped and
      reported on the remains at Saqqara — the first expedition to number the
      monuments systematically.
    from: In 1842 and 1843, as part of a Prussian expedition, [[ Karl Richard
      Lepsius ]] studied, mapped, and reported on the remains at Saqqara.[[
      ^12 ]] The expedition was the first to systematically number the
      monuments at Saqqara.[[ ^13 ]]
  - when: "1851"
    what: Auguste Mariette began excavating at Saqqara, discovering the Serapeum.
    from: From 1851 until 1855 [[ Auguste Mariette ]] excavated at Saqqara,
      discovering the Serapeum.
  - when: "1858"
    what: Mariette returned to Egypt as Director of Antiquities, and worked
      periodically at Saqqara until his death in 1881.
    from: He returned to Egypt as Director of Antiquities in 1858 and worked
      periodically at Saqqara until his death in 1881.[[ ^14 ]][[ ^15 ]][[ ^16
      ]]
  - when: 1899 to 1901
    what: Alessandro Barsanti excavated at Saqqara.
    from: "[[ Alessandro Barsanti ]] excavated here between 1899 and 1901.[[ ^20
      ]][[ ^21 ]][[ ^22 ]]"
  - when: 1905 to 1914
    what: James Edward Quibell excavated Saqqara; one of the reports was delayed
      until 1927.
    from: Saqqara was excavated between 1905 and 1914 by [[ James Edward Quibell
      ]].[[ ^23 ]][[ ^24 ]][[ ^25 ]][[ ^26 ]][[ ^27 ]][[ ^28 ]] One of the
      excavation reports was delayed until 1927.[[ ^29 ]]
  - when: "1979"
    what: The Pyramid Fields of Memphis, or Memphite Necropolis, were designated a
      World Heritage Site by UNESCO.
    from: North of the Saqqara site lies the [[ Abusir ]] pyramid complex, and to
      its south lies the [[ Dahshur ]] pyramid complex, which together with
      the [[ Giza Pyramid complex ]] to the far north comprise the Pyramid
      Fields of [[ Memphis | memphis-egypt ]], or the [[ Memphite Necropolis
      ]], which was designated as a [[ World Heritage Site ]] by [[ UNESCO ]]
      in 1979.[[ ^3 ]]
  - when: "1997"
    what: The Polish-Egyptian expedition uncovered the tomb of vizier Merefnebef,
      its funerary chapel decorated with multi-colored reliefs.
    from: The most important discoveries include the tomb of vizier [[ Merefnebef
      ]] with a funerary chapel decorated with multi-colored reliefs, which
      was uncovered in 1997.[[ ^45 ]] as well as the tomb of courtier
      Nyankhnefertem uncovered in 2003.[[ ^46 ]]
  - when: "2003"
    what: The same expedition uncovered the tomb of the courtier Nyankhnefertem.
    from: The most important discoveries include the tomb of vizier [[ Merefnebef
      ]] with a funerary chapel decorated with multi-colored reliefs, which
      was uncovered in 1997.[[ ^45 ]] as well as the tomb of courtier
      Nyankhnefertem uncovered in 2003.[[ ^46 ]]
  - when: "2011"
    what: Looters damaged Saqqara, Abusir and Dahshur during the 2011 Egyptian
      protests; store rooms were broken into, but the monuments were mostly
      unharmed.
    from: Saqqara and the surrounding areas of Abusir and Dahshur suffered damage
      by looters during the [[ 2011 Egyptian protests ]]. Store rooms were
      broken into, but the monuments were mostly unharmed.[[ ^10 ]][[ ^11 ]]
  - when: "2011"
    what: Almost eight million animal mummies were uncovered at the dog catacomb,
      next to the sacred temple of Anubis.
    from: During routine excavations in 2011 at the dog catacomb in Saqqara
      necropolis, an excavation team led by [[ Salima Ikram ]] and an
      international team of researchers led by Paul Nicholson of [[ Cardiff
      University ]] uncovered almost eight million animal mummies at the
      burial site next to the sacred temple of [[ Anubis ]].[[ ^37 ]][[ ^38 ]]
  - when: July 2018
    what: An extremely rare gilded burial mask, probably of the Saite-Persian
      period, was reported in a partly damaged wooden coffin.
    from: In July 2018, a German-Egyptian research team headed by Ramadan Badry
      Hussein of the [[ University of Tübingen ]] reported the discovery of an
      extremely rare gilded burial mask that probably dates from the
      Saite-Persian period in a partly damaged wooden [[ coffin ]].
  - when: November 2018
    what: The Egyptian government announced a previously unknown 4,400-year-old
      tomb belonging to Wahtye, a high-ranking priest under King Neferirkare
      Kakai.
    from: Also in November 2018, the Egyptian government announced the discovery
      at Saqqara of a previously unknown 4,400-year-old tomb. It belongs to [[
      Wahtye ]], a high-ranking priest[[ ^57 ]] who served under King [[
      Neferirkare Kakai ]] during the [[ Fifth Dynasty |
      fifth-dynasty-of-egypt ]], and his wife, four children and mother.[[ ^58
      ]][[ ^59 ]][[ ^60 ]][[ ^61 ]]
  - when: 13 April 2019
    what: An expedition under Mohamed Megahed discovered a 4,000-year-old tomb,
      confirmed as belonging to an influential person named Khuwy.
    from: On 13 April 2019, an expedition led by a member of the [[ Czech |
      czech-republic ]] Institute of [[ Egyptology ]], Mohamed Megahed,
      discovered a 4,000-year-old tomb near Egypt's Saqqara [[ Necropolis ]].
      Archaeologists confirmed that the tomb belonged to an influential person
      named Khuwy, who lived in Egypt during the [[ 5th Dynasty |
      fifth-dynasty-of-egypt ]].[[ ^63 ]][[ ^64 ]][[ ^65 ]][[ ^66 ]]
  - when: October 2019
    what: A cache of 30 coffins with mummies was found — at the time Egypt's
      largest in more than a century, and the first found by a solely Egyptian
      mission.
    from: In October 2019, a cache of 30 coffins with mummies was discovered, at
      the time Egypt's largest in more than a century and the first cache to
      be discovered by a solely Egyptian mission.[[ ^67 ]]
  - when: 3 October 2020
    what: Egypt's tourism and antiquities minister announced at least 59 sealed
      sarcophagi with mummies more than 2,600 years old.
    from: On 3 October 2020, Khalid el-Anany, Egypt's tourism and antiquities
      minister announced the discovery of at least 59 sealed sarcophagi with
      mummies more than 2,600 years old.
  - when: January 2021
    what: More than 50 wooden sarcophagi in 52 burial shafts of the New Kingdom
      period were announced, with a 13 ft-long papyrus carrying Chapter 17 of
      the Book of the Dead.
    from: In January 2021, the tourism and antiquities ministry announced the
      discovery of more than 50 wooden sarcophagi in 52 burial shafts which
      date back to the New Kingdom period,[[ ^80 ]][[ ^81 ]] each around 30 to
      40 feet deep,[[ ^80 ]][[ ^81 ]] and a 13 ft-long [[ papyrus ]] that
      contains texts from Chapter 17 of the //[[ Book of the Dead ]].//[[ ^81
      ]]
  - when: April 2025
    what: The tomb of Prince Waser-If-Re, son of Userkaf, was uncovered, with a
      pink granite false door 4.5 metres high.
    from: In April 2025, archaeologists led by Dr. [[ Zahi Hawass ]] uncovered the
      tomb of Prince Waser-If-Re, son of [[ Userkaf ]], the founder of [[
      Egypt's Fifth Dynasty | fifth-dynasty-of-egypt ]]. The tomb features a
      pink granite false door, measuring 4.5 meters in height and 1.15 meters
      in width, inscribed with the prince's titles, including "Hereditary
      Prince," "Royal Scribe," "Vizier," and "Chanting Priest".
