    section: new-kingdom
    caption: In the Apis Tombs of the [[ Serapeum of Saqqara ]], a passage with
      recesses that house sarcophagi for the bulls
glossary:
  - term: Necropolis
    gloss: A burial ground.
    from: "description: Burial ground in Giza Governorate, Egypt"
  - term: Markaz
    gloss: An Egyptian county; Saqqara lies in the markaz of Badrashin, in the
      Giza Governorate.
    from: is an Egyptian village in the markaz (county) of [[ Badrashin ]] in the
      [[ Giza Governorate ]],[[ ^1 ]]
  - term: Mastaba
    gloss: A kind of tomb; during the Old Kingdom it was customary for courtiers
      to be buried in mastaba tombs close to the pyramid of their king.
    from: During the Old Kingdom, it was customary for courtiers to be buried in
      mastaba tombs close to the pyramid of their king.
  - term: Pyramid Texts
    gloss: The texts with which Unas, the last ruler of the Fifth Dynasty, became
      the first king to adorn the chambers of his pyramid.
    from: "[[ Unas ]], the last ruler of the Fifth Dynasty, was the first king to
      adorn the chambers in his pyramid with [[ Pyramid Texts ]]."
  - term: Gisr el-Mudir
    gloss: A large rectangular enclosure at Saqqara, which may have been built by
      Khasekhemwy, or could belong to Nynetjer.
    from: Khasekhemwy may nonetheless also have built a funerary monument at
      Saqqara consisting of a large rectangular enclosure, known as [[ Gisr
      el-Mudir ]], although this enclosure could also belong to Nynetjer.
  - term: Serapeum
    gloss: The burial site of the mummified Apis bulls, enlarged by Prince
      Khaemweset, who was later buried in the catacombs.
    from: He enlarged the [[ Serapeum | serapeum-of-saqqara ]], the burial site of
      the mummified [[ Apis bulls | apis-egyptian-mythology ]], and was later
      buried in the catacombs.
  - term: Dry Moat
    gloss: A vast trench hewn around the Djoser Pyramid; the most recent
      discoveries confirm the hypothesis that it was a model of the pharaoh's
      journey to the netherworld.
    from: The research of the Polish-Egyptian expedition also focuses on the
      interpretation of the so-called Dry Moat, a vast trench hewn around the
      Djoser Pyramid. The most recent discoveries confirm the hypothesis that
      the Dry Moat was a model of the pharaoh's journey to the netherworld, a
      road the deceased ruler had to follow to attain eternal life.[[ ^44 ]][[
      ^50 ]][[ ^51 ]]
  - term: Ard as-Sadr
    gloss: "The name medieval authors also use for the village: Arabic for 'land
      of the buckthorn'."
    from: "Medieval authors also refer to the village as !!Ard as-Sadr!! (Arabic:
      ارض السدر, [[ lit. | literal-translation ]] 'land of the buckthorn').[[
      ^6 ]]"
further:
  - slug: memphis-egypt
    why: Saqqara was the necropolis of Memphis; from the Middle Kingdom onward
      Memphis was no longer the capital, and the kings built their funerary
      complexes elsewhere.
  - slug: abydos-egypt
    why: The royal burial ground while the earliest nobles were being buried at
      Saqqara, and where Seth-Peribsen and Khasekhemwy were buried instead.
  - slug: book-of-the-dead
    why: A 13 ft-long papyrus with texts from its Chapter 17 came out of a Saqqara
      burial shaft in January 2021, and excerpts were painted onto other
      coffins.
anchors:
  hook: Saqqara contains the oldest complete stone building complex known in
    history, the Pyramid of Djoser, built during the [[ Third Dynasty |
    third-dynasty-of-egypt ]]. Another sixteen Egyptian kings built pyramids
    at Saqqara, which are now in various states of preservation. High
    officials added private funeral monuments to this necropolis during the
    entire [[ Pharaonic period | history-of-ancient-egypt ]]. It remained an
    important complex for non-royal burials and cult ceremonies for more than
    3,000 years, well into [[ Ptolemaic | ptolemaic-dynasty ]] and [[ Roman |
    ancient-rome ]] times.
  standfirst: is an Egyptian village in the markaz (county) of [[ Badrashin ]]
    in the [[ Giza Governorate ]],[[ ^1 ]] that contains ancient mausoleum
    crypts of Egyptian royalty, serving as the [[ necropolis ]] for the [[
    ancient Egyptian | ancient-egypt ]] capital, [[ Memphis | memphis-egypt
    ]].[[ ^2 ]] Saqqara contains numerous pyramids, including the [[ Pyramid
    of Djoser ]], sometimes referred to as the Step Pyramid, and a number of
    [[ mastaba ]] tombs. Located some 30 km (19 mi) south of modern-day [[
    Cairo ]], Saqqara covers an area of around 7 by 1.5 km (4.3 by 0.9 mi).
    Saqqara contains the oldest complete stone building complex known in
    history, the Pyramid of Djoser, built during the [[ Third Dynasty |
    third-dynasty-of-egypt ]]. Another sixteen Egyptian kings built pyramids
    at Saqqara, which are now in various states of preservation. High
    officials added private funeral monuments to this necropolis during the
    entire [[ Pharaonic period | history-of-ancient-egypt ]]. It remained an
    important complex for non-royal burials and cult ceremonies for more than
    3,000 years, well into [[ Ptolemaic | ptolemaic-dynasty ]] and [[ Roman |
    ancient-rome ]] times.
